---
import { storyEyebrow, storyTitle } from '../data/stories';
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import Reconstruction from '../components/Reconstruction.astro';
import Chevron from '../components/Chevron.astro';
import LinkRow from '../components/LinkRow.astro';

const SITE = 'https://alucify.ai/';
const APP = 'https://alucify-screens.fly.dev/#/dashboard';
const FIGMA = 'https://www.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1';
const FIGMA_EMBED =
  'https://embed.figma.com/design/N7vnpK60xZfzI8luYKbevA/Alucify-Patterns?node-id=0-1&embed-host=share';
const W = '/work/alucify';

/** Where the work sits, and where it can move to as context improves. */
const allocation = [
  ['Agent-ready', 'Grounded enough for an agent to build from'],
  ['Needs clarification', 'A person has to resolve the ambiguity first'],
  ['Requires expert judgement', 'Systemic impact warrants a deeper look'],
];

const flows = [
  ['First run', ['Sign in', 'Connect source', 'Build context', 'Ready']],
  ['Artifacts', ['Dashboard', 'Artifact', 'Audit', 'Re-audit']],
  ['Coverage', ['Dashboard', 'Coverage summary', 'Decision detail']],
  ['Impact', ['Dashboard', 'Impact summary', 'Concept detail']],
];

/** Engagement results from the Alucify system, not from the interface alone. */
const evidence = [
  ['15%', 'Requirements gap identified', 'Missing intent became visible before it became code.'],
  ['97%', 'Prototype fidelity', 'Design decisions stayed connected to what was implemented.'],
  ['62.5%', 'Implementation time saved', 'Better grounding reduced avoidable interpretation and rework.'],
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title={storyTitle('/alucify')}
  metaTitle="Alucify — the control layer for agentic software development | Tyler Young"
  description="Agents generate faster than teams can specify and absorb change. Alucify models what a brownfield codebase means, then shows where context is missing, what a change would reach, and where human judgement is still required."
  eyebrow="Semantic intelligence for software teams"
  meta={[
    ['Company', 'Semantic intelligence for brownfield software'],
    ['Contribution', 'Product design · information architecture · company narrative'],
    ['Product scope', '4 connected flows · 20 documented screens'],
    ['Status', 'Active venture · shown with the founder’s agreement'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    Alucify makes complex codebases legible to people and coding agents. I
    designed the product surfaces that expose missing context, predict the
    consequences of a change, and show teams where human judgement is still
    required.
  </p>

  <LinkRow
    links={[
      { text: 'Open the working screens', href: APP },
      { text: 'Open the design file', href: FIGMA },
      { text: 'Open the company site', href: SITE },
    ]}
  />

  <p>
    Coding agents write implementation far faster than a team can specify it,
    check it, and absorb it. But commercial software is rarely a clean project.
    It is a system shaped by years of requirements, dependencies and exceptions,
    and that context is scattered: product managers hold the intent, engineers
    hold the architectural history, and the code holds another version of the
    truth. An agent that cannot see those relationships writes code that is
    reasonable on its own and wrong for the system.
  </p>

  <h2>Showing what to do next</h2>
  <p>
    Alucify turns requirements, schemas, interfaces, code and tests into one
    queryable model of the application — the AppGraph. It can hand an agent the
    part of the system a task actually touches, instead of a pile of
    undifferentiated context.
  </p>
  <p>
    That model can produce a great deal of technical evidence, and raw graphs and
    scores would only move the interpretation problem somewhere else. So the
    design job was the decision on the other side of the analysis.
    Every score opens into the evidence beneath it. Verified context is separated
    from agent inference. A finding says why it matters, not only how severe it
    is. And nothing pretends the software can make the final call.
  </p>
  <p>
    The dashboard opens on three questions — what has been created, which
    decisions are grounded, and where a change could propagate.
  </p>

  <p>
    Agent hours and token usage sit beside them as planning evidence — what the
    work is likely to cost in effort — not as a bill.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="dashboard"
    label="Dashboard"
    title="Alucify code-review dashboard reconstruction"
    caption="Three starting points, and no single score presented as a verdict."
    eager
  />
  <h2>Three analyses, one job</h2>
  <p>
    Each answers a question a team would otherwise guess at, and each ends by
    handing the decision back.
  </p>

  <h3>Where the team still has to agree</h3>
  <p>
    Coverage Analysis measures how much of a decision rests on verified context
    and how much rests on inference. Incomplete decisions sort to the top,
    because those are where an agent is most likely to invent something. A
    coverage score is not a quality grade — it says where the conversation
    should start.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="coverage"
    label="Coverage analysis"
    title="Alucify coverage-analysis reconstruction"
    caption="Every summary stays traceable to a named decision and the evidence supporting it. The sample score is computed from the 27 decisions beneath it."
  />

  <h3>What a change would touch</h3>
  <p>
    Impact Analysis runs a proposed change through the AppGraph before anyone
    writes it: how far it reaches, what could break, what data it exposes, and
    what may stop working. A team can revise the requirement while it is still
    cheap, rather than after an agent has built the wrong reading of it. Severity
    arrives as a label, a marker and a sentence together, so nobody has to infer
    what a colour means.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="impact-detail"
    label="Impact detail"
    title="Alucify impact-detail reconstruction"
    caption="A finding names the affected concept, explains the risk, and preserves a path back to the evidence."
  />

  <h3>Conventions nobody wrote down</h3>
  <p>
    Every mature codebase has conventions that most files follow and nobody wrote
    down, and they usually live in one person's head until that person leaves. Invariance Analysis recovers them from the code, then names the one
    call site that broke the pattern — 48 of 49 sibling tests pair setup with
    cleanup; one does not. Some exceptions are drift and some are intent, so the
    product supplies the evidence and stops there.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    Each analysis narrows where a person has to look. None of them decides.
  </p>

  <h2>Where the product hands the work back</h2>
  <p>
    A coverage gap means someone has to say what they meant. An impact finding
    means someone has to weigh it. So the product sorts work into three states,
    and it moves between them as the context improves — which is the point of
    measuring it.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      allocation.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    The same honesty applies to the analysis itself. It may still be running, or
    return nothing, or have gone stale when the repository moved underneath it.
    A stale audit does not sit on screen as though it were valid: the product
    says the result is out of date and puts the fix beside the admission.
  </p>

  <Reconstruction
    route="artifact-reaudit"
    label="Stale audit"
    title="Alucify stale-audit reconstruction"
    caption="The product identifies its own result as outdated and puts the corrective action beside it."
  />

  <h2>Twenty screens, four flows</h2>
  <p>
    Onboarding establishes the codebase context every later analysis depends on.
    The dashboard provides orientation. The detailed flows let the user trace
    each signal back to the evidence underneath it.
  </p>

  <div class="flows">
    <p class="sr-only">
      Four flows. First run goes from sign in, to connect source, to build
      context, to ready. The other three each begin at the dashboard: artifacts
      continues to artifact, then audit, then re-audit; coverage continues to the
      coverage summary and then a decision detail; impact continues to the impact
      summary and then a concept detail.
    </p>
    {
      flows.map(([name, steps]) => (
        <div class="flow" aria-hidden="true">
          <p class="flow-name">{name}</p>
          <ol class="flow-steps">
            {steps.map((step) => (
              <li class:list={['flow-step', { hub: step === 'Dashboard' }]}>{step}</li>
            ))}
          </ol>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <figure class="recon figma">
    <div class="recon-head">
      <span class="recon-label">Complete design file</span>
      <span class="recon-host">20 screens · 4 flows</span>
      <a class="recon-open" href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        Open in Figma<Chevron weight="bold" />
      </a>
    </div>
    <div class="recon-viewport figma-viewport">
      <p class="recon-fallback">
        Complete design file — <a href={FIGMA} target="_blank" rel="noopener">open in Figma</a>
      </p>
      <iframe
        src={FIGMA_EMBED}
        title="The Alucify design file — twenty screens across four flows"
        loading="lazy"
        referrerpolicy="no-referrer"
        allowfullscreen></iframe>
    </div>
  </figure>
  <h2>What it changed</h2>
  <p>
    On the LangBuilder engagement, analysis identified a requirements gap before
    implementation, AppGraph-grounded prototyping held fidelity between prototype
    and product, and the team reported time saved during implementation.
  </p>

  <ul class="cells three">
    {
      evidence.map(([figure, label, why]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-figure">{figure}</span>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
          <span class="cell-note">{why}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    Those figures come from the Alucify system and the whole engagement, not from
    the interface design alone.
  </p>
  <h2>What I did</h2>
  <p>
    I was the product designer. The product sits between complex machine
    analysis and consequential human decisions, and my job was to make that
    boundary legible: what the system knows, what it inferred, what a change
    could reach, and where a person still has to step in.
  </p>
  <p>
    I designed the company website and its narrative too. Alucify sits between
    code intelligence, development infrastructure and agent governance — a
    category with no name to borrow — so the story opens on a constraint the
    reader already recognises, then shows the model underneath it.
  </p>

  <p class="pull-line">
    Alucify makes the codebase, the uncertainty, and the consequences of a change
    easier to see.
  </p>

  <div class="disclosure">
    <p>
      The interactive product screens are a working reconstruction of my designs,
      built so the states can be explored rather than viewed only as screenshots.
      The reconstruction is not the shipping product.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tenant, people, and measurements in it are invented. The sample
      summaries reconcile with their underlying rows but are not customer
      results.
    </p>
    <p>
      The LangBuilder figures are company-level engagement results. The public
      website currently reflects the company’s earlier positioning; the narrative
      described above is the current one.
    </p>
    <p>This work is shown with the founder’s agreement.</p>
  </div>
</DocumentLayout>
